§§ `Assertions -- Chapter 4A, the right of private defence`

-- s 96 with ss 97(a), 101 and 103: the right, exercised below the death ceiling.
#ASSERT `a private defence justification is made out` `defending the body without killing`
-- s 103: the same facts plus a killing fall outside the right.
#ASSERT NOT (`a private defence justification is made out` `killing the assailant with no section 102 description`)
-- s 102(a): the killing is within the right once death was reasonably apprehended.
#ASSERT `a private defence justification is made out` `killing the assailant under section 102(a)`
-- s 98(2): no right at all where a public authority could have been resorted to.
#ASSERT NOT (`a private defence justification is made out` `defending the body with recourse to a public authority available`)
-- s 98(1): no right where more harm than necessary is inflicted.
#ASSERT NOT (`a private defence justification is made out` `defending the body with more harm than necessary`)
-- s 99: the right survives the assailant's incapacity, with no separate offence element.
#ASSERT `a private defence justification is made out` `defending against an assailant of unsound mind`
-- s 106A(1): the bar bites against a public servant known to be one.
#ASSERT NOT (`a private defence justification is made out` `defending against a public servant acting in good faith`)
-- s 106A(1): and does not bite where death or grievous hurt is reasonably apprehended.
#ASSERT `a private defence justification is made out` `defending against a public servant who threatens death`
-- s 104(2): the property right ends when the thief has effected his retreat.
#ASSERT NOT (`a private defence justification is made out` `defending property against a thief who has got away`)
#ASSERT `a private defence justification is made out` `defending property against a thief still at it`
-- s 105(1)(a) and s 106: death is within the right against robbery, not against theft.
#ASSERT `a private defence justification is made out` `killing a robber`
#ASSERT NOT (`a private defence justification is made out` `killing a thief`)

§§ `Assertions -- Chapter 5, sections 111, 113 and 114`

-- s 111 with its proviso, on Illustration (a).
#ASSERT `the abettor is liable under section 111 for the different act done` `instigation where a different act was done`
-- Illustration (b): a distinct act is not a probable consequence, so s 111 does not reach it.
#ASSERT NOT (`the abettor is liable under section 111 for the different act done` `instigation where a distinct act was done`)
-- s 113 turns on the knowledge proviso, and fails without it.
#ASSERT `the abettor is liable under section 113 for the different effect caused` `instigation causing an effect the abettor knew was likely`
#ASSERT NOT (`the abettor is liable under section 113 for the different effect caused` `instigation causing an unforeseen effect`)
-- s 114: presence converts abetment into principal liability.
#ASSERT `the abettor is deemed to have committed the act or offence under section 114` `abettor present at the commission`
-- and does not fire on an abettor who was absent.
#ASSERT NOT (`the abettor is deemed to have committed the act or offence under section 114` `instigation where a different act was done`)

§§ `Assertions -- the private defence wiring, and Chapter 1 sections 3, 4A and 5`

-- The point of the rewiring: a taking that satisfies s 378 in every element is not
-- theft when it is done in the exercise of the right of private defence (s 6).
#ASSERT `constitutes theft` (`disarming an assailant`'s `property taking`) (`disarming an assailant`'s `fault`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the proposed act constitutes theft` `disarming an assailant`)
#ASSERT `the proposed act constitutes theft` `taking a knife with no defence`
#ASSERT NOT (`any screened offence is indicated` `disarming an assailant`)
#ASSERT `any screened offence is indicated` `taking a knife with no defence`
-- s 6 reports the Chapter 4A route as well as the Chapter 4 one.
#ASSERT `the act is taken outside offence by Chapter 4 or Chapter 4A` `disarming an assailant`
#ASSERT NOT (`a general exception applies` `disarming an assailant`)
-- s 3, s 4A: both bring a foreign act within the territorial screen.
#ASSERT `the act is dealt with under this Code as if committed within Singapore` `act abroad by a person triable in Singapore`
#ASSERT `Singapore has territorial reach over the proposed act` `act abroad by a person triable in Singapore`
#ASSERT `the act is deemed committed in Singapore under section 4A` `genocide abroad by a citizen`
#ASSERT `Singapore has territorial reach over the proposed act` `genocide abroad by a citizen`
-- and neither fires on an act wholly within Singapore with neither fact asserted.
#ASSERT NOT (`the act is dealt with under this Code as if committed within Singapore` `innocent act in Singapore`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the act is deemed committed in Singapore under section 4A` `innocent act in Singapore`)
-- s 5: the saving is reported, and no encoded offence is indicated on the same facts.
#ASSERT `another written law is unaffected and may still apply` `conduct also punishable under another written law`
#ASSERT NOT (`any screened offence is indicated` `conduct also punishable under another written law`)

#EVAL `the offence screen of` `disarming an assailant`
